---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { ConvertImageTool } from "../../../components/image/ConvertImageTool.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/image/webp-to-png")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Convert a WebP file to PNG, preserving any transparency, for compatibility with tools that don't support WebP."
>
  <ConvertImageTool client:load acceptAttr="image/webp" acceptedLabel="WebP" outputFormat="png" />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Select a WebP file. It's decoded in your browser and re-encoded as PNG — nothing is uploaded unless you switch
      to the server option. Transparency is preserved exactly.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 600×600 WebP icon with a transparent background converts to a 600×600 PNG with the same transparent
      background intact.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>PNG encoding here is lossless — pixel data is preserved exactly, though PNG files are often larger than the equivalent WebP.</li>
      <li>Only the first frame of an animated WebP is converted; animation is not supported.</li>
      <li>Embedded metadata (such as ICC color profiles) from the source file is not carried over.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Accepted inputs</h2>
    <p>One WebP, up to 25 MB and about 40 million pixels. Animation is not kept; only the first frame is converted.</p>

    <h2>When the file leaves your device</h2>
    <p>
      Choosing a file and seeing the preview keeps it on your device. It stays there while "Process on your device (default)" is selected. The file is sent only if you select "Upload to our server for processing" and then press Convert. The server holds that file in memory for the one request and does not write it to disk. A failure on your device does not upload the file by itself.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
